Subject: [PATCH] libnvdimm: finish registering boot-time devices before /init

nd_device_register() hands region and namespace devices to an exclusive
async domain, which the async_synchronize_full() in kernel_init() does
not wait for. With the root filesystem on an e820 (legacy PMEM) region
and the initramfs's /init mounting it straight away, open("/dev/pmem0")
races the namespace probe and fails with ENXIO.

Wait for the nd domain at late_initcall_sync, which runs before
kernel_init() goes on to execute /init. Synchronizing a domain also waits
for work queued while it waits (a region's namespaces are scheduled from
the region's own registration), so every boot-time namespace has its
block device by then.

--- a/drivers/nvdimm/bus.c	2026-10-30 13:06:41.000000000 +0100
+++ b/drivers/nvdimm/bus.c	2026-11-04 12:14:34.002418223 +0100
@@ -483,6 +483,22 @@
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(nd_synchronize);
 
+/*
+ * Region and namespace devices are registered (and probed) from
+ * nd_async_domain, which is exclusive, so kernel_init()'s
+ * async_synchronize_full() doesn't wait for it: without this, /init can
+ * run before /dev/pmemN exists. Registering a region schedules its
+ * namespaces from the same domain, and synchronizing a domain waits for
+ * work queued while it waits, so this returns with every boot-time
+ * namespace probed.
+ */
+static int __init nd_synchronize_before_init(void)
+{
+	nd_synchronize();
+	return 0;
+}
+late_initcall_sync(nd_synchronize_before_init);
+
 static void nd_async_device_register(void *d, async_cookie_t cookie)
 {
 	struct device *dev = d;
